The lifecycle and pytest fixture design references shibukawa/pgmem,
Copyright (c) 2026 Yoshiki Shibukawa, MIT License. Its notice is included.
The project also takes inspiration from pg-mem and PGlite's testing experience;
their database implementations are not included in the mariamem Python code.

The distribution is intended for testing. Prepared authentication keys
are public, non-secret test material, never production credentials. Exact
source, notice and platform verification remains required for every release
before public distribution. See docs/releasing.md and THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.

The v0.4 default direct-links generated Go in Go consumers and
links it into the host-only Python wheel. WASM is a build intermediate; normal
execution does not include Wasmer/AOT runtime assets. It uses goccy/wasm2go via the
shibukawa/wasm2go-fork commit ac98bcf00c17d8531f0c071a9836d0b50975e7ff.
Its preserved MIT notice is in licenses/wasm2go-MIT.txt (Masaaki Goshima).
This does not remove MariaDB/lite4mariadb corresponding-source obligations.

The translated guest retains WASIX libc (including musl, cloudlibc and dlmalloc),
LLVM compiler-rt/libc++/libc++abi/libunwind, wolfSSL, zlib, PCRE2 and fmt code.
Translation to Go does not remove their original terms. Preserve their texts
in licenses/ and their pinned sources in the corresponding-source archive.
wolfSSL's upstream LICENSING permits the GPLv2 election for combination with
MariaDB Server; this distribution uses that election and preserves COPYING
and LICENSING. The Python host also contains the Go runtime (BSD-3-Clause).

Legacy Wasmer fallback is separate: its engine, Singlepass BUSL-1.1 and Rust
dependency notices are preserved in the repository for legacy bundles, not
included in normal host-only wheel notices. The Go module/corresponding-source
package still carries that fallback's source and historical notices; it does
not bundle the Wasmer engine. See release/distribution-licenses.json and
docs/v04-license-inventory.md for the artifact-specific inventory.
